            <div xml:id="d73">

                <pb facs="0225" n="111" xml:id="pg111"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">462.00R296/4200: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Acting Secretary of
                            State to the Ambassador in Germany</hi> (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">Sackett</hi>)</head>
                <opener><seg rendition="#center">[Paraphrase]</seg></opener>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1931-06-30T14:00:00-04:00">June 30, 1931—2
                    p.m.</date></dateline></opener>
                <p>98. This telegram refers to our telephone conversation. Yesterday our Embassy in
                    Paris reported that in the session of the French Senate today the Minister of
                    Marine intends to say that the building of the 23,000-ton cruiser which the
                    French had originally included in their naval appropriation for this coming year
                    will be abandoned provided that Germany will accordingly halt its construction
                    of vessels of the <hi rendition="#italic">Deutschland</hi> type. I trust that
                    for their part the German Government will deem it possible to answer by
                    abandoning such construction.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Castle</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
